{#
   THE CATALOGUE CARD — one card shape for every kind of thing in a list.

   ⚠️ The layout rule this card exists to enforce: **availability and permission
   are different KINDS of fact and are separated by POSITION, not by colour.**
   The head says what is true of the *thing* (libre, occupée, emprunté, fermé);
   the foot says what is true of *you* (libre-service, formation requise, à
   rendre le 12/08). A member who reads "indisponible" when the truth is "pas
   formé" waits for something that is never going to happen.

   ⚠️ This template computes NOTHING. It does not know what a machine is, and it
   must not learn. The caller decides the state word, its tone, and the footer's
   words; this file positions and colours them. An `{% if %}` on entity type
   here is the bug that ends the whole exercise.

   Parameters:

     url          where the card goes
     title        the thing's identity — the headline
     type         the small uppercase line under it (its category/kind)
     meta         one quiet line (location, owner, …)
     note         one more quiet line, usually the "when" (next slot, due date)
     note_strong  the emphasised fragment inside `note`, appended after it

     state        the word: "Libre", "Emprunté", "Labo fermé"
     state_tone   free | busy | down | closed — colour only, never meaning

     foot         the permission/obligation word
     foot_tone    a SIGNAL name, same vocabulary as state_tone.
                  ⚠️ `go` (green) is only correct on a card that draws NO head
                  state chip, or the two greens collide — see the note beside
                  .ml-req-ok in machines-list.css.
     action       the call to action ("Voir")

     icon_slug    which category icon to draw (head + media fallback)
     photo        an image path; the media box keeps the SAME aspect ratio with
                  or without one, so a half-photographed catalogue stays even
#}
{#
   ⚠️ Two extension points, added when "Mes réservations" adopted this card. Both
   exist so there is still exactly ONE card in the codebase — a second card copied
   from this one is two things to restyle and two things to drift.

     url      may now be EMPTY. A card that leads nowhere renders the same box
              without an anchor, instead of an `<a href="">` that reloads the page.
     actions  a block AFTER the anchor and inside the article, for interactive
              controls — a cancel form, a button. ⚠️ It must stay outside the
              anchor: a `<form>` or `<button>` nested in an `<a>` is invalid HTML
              and browsers resolve it unpredictably. That constraint is the whole
              reason this block exists rather than another parameter.
#}
{#
   THE SIGNAL VOCABULARY — callers name the MEANING, this file picks the colour.

   Red, amber and green mean the same thing to everyone because of traffic
   lights, and the card already followed that by accident. What it did not have
   was a way to *say* so: callers passed `foot_tone: 'yes'` for amber and
   `'ok'` for green, while the head used `'free'` and `'down'`. Nothing in those
   names tells you what signal you are sending, which is exactly how the badges
   list ended up hardcoded to grey for both "obtenu" and "à obtenir" without
   anyone noticing.

     go       green   available · achieved · you may proceed
     wait     blue    taken right now, by someone else. Not a warning: nothing
                      is wrong, it is simply not your turn.
     caution  amber   you must do something first (train, qualify, return)
     stop     red     out of service · refused
     muted    grey    neutral · closed · not yet started

   ⚠️ **Amber is never "busy" and blue is never "warning".** Both halves of the
   card can now emit both, and using amber for occupancy would put two ambers on
   one card meaning different things: "someone is using it" and "you are not
   allowed to". That is the mistake the position rule already guards against,
   restated in colour.

   ⚠️ The two halves keep separate class prefixes because the CSS differs, but
   the vocabulary is one. A caller should never write a class name. The head's
   half of the mapping lives in `_state_chip.html.twig`, which the booking page
   also uses; the map below is the foot's.
#}
{% set _signal = {
    'go':      'ok',
    'wait':    'wait',
    'caution': 'yes',
    'stop':    'stop',
    'muted':   'none',
} %}
{% set _foot_class = _signal[foot_tone|default('muted')]|default('none') %}

<article class="ml-card">
    <{{ url|default('') ? 'a' : 'div' }}{% if url|default('') %} href="{{ url }}"{% endif %} class="ml-card-link">

        {# ⚠️ The head is skipped entirely when it would be empty. A caller that
           passes neither an icon nor a state (formations did) was getting an 18px
           bar containing `<span></span>` — a visible empty strip above the media
           box that reads as a rendering fault. The `<span></span>` placeholder is
           still needed when only ONE of the two is present, to keep the state chip
           pushed right by `justify-content: space-between`. #}
        {% if icon_slug|default('') or state|default('') %}
            <div class="ml-card-head">
                {% if icon_slug|default('') %}
                    <span class="ml-card-cat" title="{{ type|default('') }}">
                        {% include 'site/_category_icon.html.twig' with { slug: icon_slug } only %}
                    </span>
                {% else %}
                    <span></span>
                {% endif %}

                {% include 'site/_state_chip.html.twig' with {
                    label: state|default(''),
                    signal: state_tone|default('muted'),
                } only %}
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        <div class="ml-media">
            {% if photo|default('') %}
                <img src="{{ photo starts with 'http' or photo starts with '/' ? photo : asset(photo) }}"
                     alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
            {% else %}
                <span class="ml-media-icon">
                    {% include 'site/_category_icon.html.twig' with { slug: icon_slug|default('') } only %}
                </span>
            {% endif %}
        </div>

        <div class="ml-card-body">
            <h2 class="ml-name">{{ title }}</h2>
            {% if type|default('') %}<p class="ml-type">{{ type }}</p>{% endif %}
            {% if meta|default('') %}<p class="ml-loc">{{ meta }}</p>{% endif %}
            {% if note|default('') %}
                <p class="ml-next">{{ note }}{% if note_strong|default('') %} <strong>{{ note_strong }}</strong>{% endif %}</p>
            {% endif %}
        </div>

        <div class="ml-card-foot">
            {% if foot|default('') %}
                <span class="ml-req ml-req-{{ _foot_class }}">{{ foot }}</span>
            {% else %}
                <span></span>
            {% endif %}
            {% if action|default('') %}<span class="ml-see">{{ action }}</span>{% endif %}
        </div>
    </{{ url|default('') ? 'a' : 'div' }}>

    {% block actions %}{% endblock %}
</article>
